A||||12||Dslice||Temp-Gauge problem||||9||
Z||000000||Dslice||06-19-2001||02:35 PM||||On my 86GT the temp gauge is malfuctioning.<BR>On startup the needle jumps wildly and when the car is running it's pegged at zero and doesnt move after warm up. Im not getting a code 14 or 15. has anyone seen this happen. Could it be a wiring problem or maybe the temp sensor has gone bad?<P>thanx,<BR>Rick<BR>86GT<BR>84coup <A HREF="http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt" TARGET=_blank>http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt</A> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||rockcrawl||06-19-2001||05:06 PM||||It is a known wiring problem from the factory. Do a search and you will find the fix. It requires cutting and splicing a wire under the dash. I did it to my GT and it worked great.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||06-19-2001||06:36 PM||||Before you do that though..change your temp sensor. Cheap part at Autozone (or wherever) and 5 minute fix. Worked for me. M7F||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrgone||06-19-2001||08:07 PM||||im having the same prob, wheres the temp sensor located?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dslice||06-19-2001||11:47 PM||||The temp sensor is located next to the thermostat housing. Its sorta burried below some vaccumm hoses. <BR>I think there might be some kinda short to the gauge. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroluke||06-20-2001||07:59 AM||||Keep your temp sensors apart, or you may end up replacing the wrong one! The gauge temp sensor is NOT next to the thermostat housing. Here's a short list of the different temp sensors on the Fiero:<P>There is one for the ECM (which sets a code when broken, but not if it's inaccurate!), located next to the thermostat housing<P>One for the gauges (in the head near the distributor)<P>One for the air temp (V6: air cleaner housing, 87/88 L4: intake manifold, passenger side)<P>One for the cold start valve (V6 only, near thermostat housing)<P>One for the fan (except for the 87/88 L4), also located near the thermostat housing (at least on the V6)<P>If you're not specific, the parts counter guy may give you the wrong part, so watch out.<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000006||Dslice||06-20-2001||01:01 PM||||Thanks Oliver, you saved me. I was wondering why the temp sensor I was looking at only had one wire coming from it. I just found a picture of the sensor locations, at least the ones by the thermostat housing. Thank goodness I dont have mess with those yet. There tough to get at. And I hoping it just the gauge sensor. I really hate looking for bad wiring.<P>Thanks again!<P>Rick<BR>86GT<BR>84COUP||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bob Englert||06-20-2001||01:22 PM||||You can check for proper operation of the gauge wiring and the gauge itself by shorting the leads to the block with the ignition on.  One of the leads runs the gauge needle itself, the other operates the warning light.  When you short the lead to the block the light comes on and the needle swings to full scale.<P>Often the connector is destroyed due to high heat in the area.  AC/Delco makes replacement connectors for the sensor, just be sure to determine which is which (gauge-warning light).  You can determine with lead on the sensor is for the gauge by measuring the resistance of each pin.  The gauge will be measurable, the warming light will be open until the warming temp is reached.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 06-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dslice||06-20-2001||03:44 PM||||Ok, I found the temp sensor for the ECM, but I can't find the the one for the gauge on the V6. Is it to the left, right, above or below the Distributor. There's so much crap in the way. Will the fan switch still turn the fan on even if the other sensors or gauge is malfuctioning when the engine runs too hot?<P>Thanks again guys,<P>Rick<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dslice||06-20-2001||09:29 PM||||Oh never mind I found the sensor it was right in front of my face. Boy I feel dumb.<P>Rick<BR>86GT<BR>84Coup <A HREF="http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt" TARGET=_blank>http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroluke||06-21-2001||03:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dslice:<BR><B>Will the fan switch still turn the fan on even if the other sensors or gauge is malfuctioning when the engine runs too hot?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, the switch is completely autonomous (IF it is working). If it's broken, you likely won't notice, you may just overheat the engine! If you notice in time, you can turn on the A/C (if you have A/C) to turn on the fan manually. But before that, no sign, because the fan switch closes at 235 F or something, really late anyway. Rodney Dickman sells lower temp fan switches that I'd really like to try out...<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<BR><P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Denny||06-21-2001||07:59 AM||||Oliver (and others),<P>I installed the Hypertech 176° fan switch, available from The Fiero Store, in both of my 86's (without the low-temp thermostat) a couple of years ago, and I never worry about the engine temperature anymore. I'm aware of the argument about it shortening fan life, but I have AC in both anyway, and use it a good bit, so I figure it won't shorten it that much.<P>BTW, I have the "pegging" (wrap-around) temperature gauge problem in my GT, going to try Oliver's fix (http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/tempgauge/tempgauge.htm).<P>Thanks, Oliver!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dslice||06-24-2001||08:50 PM||||Final note on the temp gauge, turned out the needle just came loose and and finally came off.  I pulled the whole pod apart and pushed the needle back on.<P>Rick<BR>86GT<BR>84COUP||||reg||1
